Here's a possible implementation of the recursive Gauss Elimination function with partial pivoting according to Task #1:

```MATLAB
function x = LastNameFirstInit_Lab7(A, b)
    % Get matrix dimensions
    n = size(A, 1);

    % Check if this is the terminal instance
    if n == 1
        x = b / A; % Solve the single equation
    else
        % Perform partial pivoting
        [~, pivot_index] = max(abs(A(:, 1))); 
        A([1, pivot_index], :) = A([pivot_index, 1], :);
        b([1, pivot_index]) = b([pivot_index, 1]);

        % Get the new A_prime and b_prime
        A_prime = A(2:end, 2:end) - A(2:end, 1) * A(1, 2:end) / A(1, 1);
        b_prime = b(2:end) - b(1) * A(2:end, 1) / A(1, 1);

        % Recursion step: solve for the smaller system
        x_recursive = LastNameFirstInit_Lab7(A_prime, b_prime);

        % Calculate the first element of x vector based on the smaller system solution
        x1 = (b(1) - A(1, 2:end) * x_recursive) / A(1, 1);

        % Combine the results to form the final solution vector
        x = [x1; x_recursive];
    end
end
```

Please replace `LastNameFirstInit` with your actual last name and first initial. This function takes the input [A] matrix and [b] column vector and returns the [x] solution vector for the given system of linear algebraic equations. Keep in mind that this is just one possible implementation and there might be other ways to achieve the same goal.

