Aim:
The aim of this project is to implement the Gauss Elimination method for solving a system of linear equations and to assess its computational efficiency and accuracy.

Objective:
1. To understand the Gauss Elimination method and its applicability to solving a system of linear equations.
2. To develop an algorithm for implementing the Gauss Elimination method.
3. To implement the method in MATLAB for a given system of linear equations.
4. To evaluate the efficiency of the method using execution time and memory requirements.
5. To interpret the results and the reliability of the Gauss Elimination method.

Algorithm:
1. Take input for the augmented matrix representing the system of linear equations.
2. Loop through each row of the augmented matrix.
   a. Ensure the pivot element is not zero.
   b. Perform row operations to make all elements below the pivot element zero.
   c. If necessary, swap rows to find a non-zero pivot element.
3. Perform back substitution to obtain the solution of linear equations.
4. Return the solution of the system of linear equations.

MATLAB Code:

```MATLAB
% Gauss Elimination Method
clc; clear all; close all;

% Define the augmented matrix representing the system of linear equations
% Sample system: x + y + z = 6, 2y + 5z = -4, x + y - z = 27
A = [1 1 1 6;
     0 2 5 -4;
     1 1 -1 27];

% Gauss Elimination method implementation
n = size(A, 1);

% Forward elimination
for k = 1:n-1
    for i = k+1:n
        factor = A(i, k) / A(k, k);
        A(i, k:n+1) = A(i, k:n+1) - factor * A(k, k:n+1);
    end
end

% Back substitution
x = zeros(n, 1);
x(n) = A(n, n+1) / A(n, n);

for i = n-1:-1:1
    x(i) = (A(i, n+1) - A(i, i+1:n) * x(i+1:n)) / A(i, i);
end

% Print the solutions for the system of linear equations
fprintf('The solutions are: \n');
disp(x);

% Graph: Residual Error vs Variables
% Calculate the residual error using A * x - b
residual_error = abs(A(:, 1:n) * x - A(:, n+1));

figure;
bar(1:n, residual_error);
xlabel('Variable Index');
ylabel('Residual Error');
title('Gauss Elimination Method: Residual Error Vs Variables');
grid on;
```

Graph Interpretation:
The graph of residual error versus variables shows the accuracy of the Gauss Elimination method in computing the solution to the system of linear equations. Lower residual errors imply better solutions and indicate the reliability of the method.

Interpretation:
