The aim of this project is to implement the Newton-Raphson method to find the roots of a given equation and analyze its convergence.

Objective:
1. To understand the mathematical concept behind Newton-Raphson method.
2. To develop an algorithm for implementing the Newton-Raphson method.
3. To implement the method in MATLAB for a given equation.
4. To analyze the convergence of the method using graphs.
5. To interpret the efficiency of the method.

Algorithm:
1. Define an initial guess for the root.
2. Define the given equation and its derivative with respect to the variable.
3. Calculate the next approximation for the root using the Newton-Raphson formula.
4. Repeat steps 3 and 4 until the difference between successive approximations is less than a predefined tolerance level.

MATLAB Code:

```MATLAB
% Newton-Raphson Method
clc; clear all; close all;

% Define the given equation and its derivative
f = @(x) x^3 - x^2 - 1; % Sample equation: x^3 - x^2 - 1 = 0
f_prime = @(x) 3*x^2 - 2*x; % Derivative: 3x^2 - 2x

% Initial guess for the root
x0 = 1;

% Tolerance level
tolerance = 1e-6;

% Maximum number of iterations
max_iterations = 100;

% Newton-Raphson method implementation
x = x0; % Initialize x
error = 1;
iter = 0;

% Store values for graph plotting
x_vals = [];
errors = [];

while error > tolerance && iter < max_iterations
    x_new = x - f(x) / f_prime(x);
    x_vals = [x_vals, x_new];
    error = abs(x_new - x);
    errors = [errors, error];
    x = x_new;
    iter = iter + 1;
end

% Print the final approximation for the root
fprintf('The root is approximately: %f\n', x);

% Plot the error versus iteration graph
figure;
plot(1:iter, errors, 'b-o', 'LineWidth', 2);
xlabel('Iterations');
ylabel('Error');
title('Newton-Raphson Method: Error Vs Iterations');
grid on;
```

